<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
    Launcher glyph: the Pi mark, drawn as pixels.

    This is the geometry of pi.dev's own logo (viewBox 800x800, two right-angle
    polygons), scaled by 0.080. Everything is axis-aligned rectangles, which is
    what gives it the pixel-face look at launcher size — the stem of the P, the
    notch that forms the bowl, and the detached square that dots the i.

    **The group's translation is what centres it, and it is not cosmetic.** The
    mark is asymmetric: the bowl of the P ends at y 58.8 while the i's dot runs
    down to y 77.6, so its bounding box is 37.6 wide by 37.6 tall with its centre
    at (48.7, 58.8). The adaptive-icon viewport centres on (54, 54), so an
    untranslated draw sits 5.3 units left and 4.8 units low — visible on a
    launcher, where the mask makes the offset obvious. Translating the whole
    group by (+5.3, -4.8) puts the bounding box exactly on the centre; the raw
    path data is left alone so it still matches the source geometry one-to-one
    and can be re-scaled without re-deriving it.

    One path with evenOdd fill rather than two: the bowl of the P is a genuine
    hole, and drawing it as a second filled path would leave a visible seam
    wherever the two met on a low-density screen.

    **White, on black, with no colour of its own.** The mark was pi's amber until
    it was looked at on a launcher rather than in the source: at 48–64dp a single
    flat shape is what reads, the icons around it are monochrome, and an amber
    glyph is then the only tinted thing in the row. White has a second effect —
    the themed-icon layer (`ic_launcher_monochrome`, which the system recolours)
    is now the same picture as this one, so the icon is recognisable in either
    mode. The amber is not gone, it moved to where it is an interface accent
    rather than a logo: `ic_pi_mark.xml` and the setup screen's progress bar.

    `tools/render-icon.py` derives the README's copy of this icon from this file
    and `values/colors.xml`, and `tools/build-apks.py` runs it in check mode, so
    the picture at the top of the README cannot drift away from the app's icon.
    (No double hyphen in an XML comment: the check flag's name is spelled out in
    that script's own docstring instead, because `aapt2` refuses a comment
    containing one — which is how this paragraph was written the second time.)
-->
<vector xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android"
    android:width="108dp"
    android:height="108dp"
    android:viewportWidth="108"
    android:viewportHeight="108">
    <group android:translateX="5.3" android:translateY="-4.8">
        <path
            android:fillColor="#FFFFFFFF"
            android:fillType="evenOdd"
            android:pathData="M29.9,40 L58.1,40 L58.1,58.8 L48.7,58.8 L48.7,68.2 L39.3,68.2 L39.3,77.6 L29.9,77.6 Z M39.3,49.4 L39.3,58.8 L48.7,58.8 L48.7,49.4 Z" />
        <path
            android:fillColor="#FFFFFFFF"
            android:pathData="M58.1,58.8 L67.5,58.8 L67.5,77.6 L58.1,77.6 Z" />
    </group>
</vector>
